<h2 id=3D"intuition---dynamic-programming">Intuition - Dynamic Programming<=
/h2>
<p>In the previous approach, it's important to note that by the time we rea=
ch integer <code>x</code>, we have already computed the result for all inte=
gers from 0 to <code>x - 1</code>. If we find a way to leverage these previ=
ous results, we can improve the efficiency of constructing the output array=
.</p>
<p>It would be wise to find a way to take advantage of some <strong>optimal=
 substructure</strong> by treating the results from integers 0 to <code>x -=
 1</code> as potential subproblems of <code>x</code>. This is the beginning=
 of a DP solution. Let <code>dp[x]</code> represent the number of set bits =
in integer <code>x</code>.</p>
<p>A predictable way to access a subproblem of <code>dp[x]</code> is to rig=
ht-shift <code>x</code> by 1, effectively removing its LSB. This is the sub=
problem <code>dp[x &gt;&gt; 1]</code>, and the only difference between this=
 and <code>dp[x]</code> is the LSB which was just removed.</p>
<p>As mentioned earlier, the LSB of <code>x</code> can be found using <code=
>x &amp; 1</code>. Therefore:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>If the LSB of <code>x</code> is 0, there is no difference in the number =
of bits between <code>x</code> and <code>x &gt;&gt; 1</code>:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a diagram illustrating a bitwise right shif=
t operation.  The top row shows an initial value `x` represented by two enc=
ircled '1's, followed by three '0's.  A blue arrow labeled 'LSB' points dow=
nwards, indicating the least significant bit is being processed.  Grey arro=
ws connect each bit in the top row to the corresponding bit in the bottom r=
ow.  The top row represents the initial value of `x`, and the bottom row sh=
ows the result of the right shift operation (`x &gt;&gt; 1`).  The encircle=
d '1's in the top row shift one position to the right, resulting in the enc=
ircled '1's in the bottom row. The remaining bits are filled with '0's.  To=
 the right, `dp[x] =3D 2` indicates the initial count of '1's in `x`, and `=
dp[x &gt;&gt; 1] =3D 2` shows the count of '1's after the right shift.  The=
 diagram visually demonstrates how the right shift operation moves bits to =
the right, filling empty positions with zeros, and how this affects the cou=
nt of '1's." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"432" height=3D"105" decoding=3D"asyn=
