 LSB '1'.  Grey arrows show the right bit-shift operation (`&gt;&gt; 1`), r=
esulting in a new binary number `011`. These shifted bits are also enclosed=
 in orange circles.  The text `x =3D 1 1 0` labels the original binary numb=
er, while `x &gt;&gt; 1 =3D 0 1 1` labels the result of the right bit shift=
.  The expressions `dp[x] =3D 3` and `dp[x &gt;&gt; 1] =3D 2` suggest that =
`dp` is an array or function that counts the number of '1's in the binary r=
epresentation of `x` and `x &gt;&gt; 1`, respectively.  The values 3 and 2 =
reflect the number of '1's in the original and shifted binary numbers.  The=
 zeros in the diagram represent bits with a value of zero." loading=3D"lazy=
" width=3D"432" height=3D"108" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"ht=
tps://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/bit-manipulation/hammin=
g-weights-of-integers/image-18-01-5-S56YEFKH.svg" style=3D"color: transpare=
nt;"></div></figure>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Therefore, we can obtain <code>dp[x]</code> by using the result of <code=
>dp[x &gt;&gt; 1]</code> and adding the LSB to it:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>dp[x] =3D dp[x &gt;&gt; 1] + (x &amp; 1)</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Now, we just need to know what our base case is.</p>
<p><strong>Base case</strong><br>
The simplest version of this problem is when <code>n</code> is 0. In this c=
ase, there are no set bits, so the number of set bits is 0. We can apply th=
is base case by setting <code>dp[0]</code> to 0.</p>
<p>After the base case is set, we populate the rest of the DP array by appl=
ying our formula from <code>dp[1]</code> to <code>dp[n]</code>. The answer =
to the problem is then just the values in the DP array, containing the numb=
er of set bits for each number from 0 to <code>n</code>.</p>
